5 Public Commands
*****************

This chapter documents every interactive command in the package.

 -- Command: mab-org-create-project project-number project-tag
          project-title &optional base-dir bib-path
     Stamp out a new project at
     ‘BASE/mab<PROJECT-NUMBER>/mab<PROJECT-NUMBER>.org’.

     PROJECT-NUMBER is the numeric project identifier (for example
     ‘"0386"’).  PROJECT-TAG is the short tag that follows the number in
     the document title.  PROJECT-TITLE is the longer human-readable
     title used in the running header.  BIB-PATH is the path passed to
     LaTeX ‘\bibliography’; when nil, ‘mab-org-global-bib-file’ is used
     (with the ‘.bib’ suffix stripped).

     The function refuses to overwrite an existing project file.

     Interactive synopsis:

          M-x mab-org-create-project RET 0386 RET mpQcr RET multipass QCr RET

 -- Command: mab-org-open-file project-number &optional base-dir
     Find or create a mab project file by PROJECT-NUMBER.  When
     ‘BASE/mab<PROJECT-NUMBER>/mab<PROJECT-NUMBER>.org’ already exists
     it is opened.  Otherwise the user is asked whether to create it
     from the template, and if so prompted for a project tag and title.

 -- Command: mab-org-wrap-citekey
     Replace the citekey under the cursor with LaTeX-wrapped text and
     create an empty per-citekey Org note when one does not yet exist.
     Works with ‘[cite:@KEY]’, plain LaTeX style, or bare-word citekeys.
     The wrapped block contains ‘\bibentry{KEY}’,
     ‘\addcontentsline{toc}{subsubsection}’, an ‘#+INCLUDE:’ directive,
     a bare file link to the per-note Org file, and a single PDF link.
     The PDF link points at ‘mab-org-pdfs-directory’ by default, or at
     ‘mab-org-books-directory’ when the BibTeX entry for the citekey
     matches ‘mab-org-book-entry-regexp’.  The links are not wrapped in
     a Notes drawer, so they remain visible in the exported document.

     When a note for the citekey already exists, the user is prompted to
     (r)euse, create a (v)ariant, or (q)uit.  *Note Variant
     Resolution::.

 -- Command: mab-org-wrap-citekey-abib
     Same as ‘mab-org-wrap-citekey’, with two differences.  First, the
     project ‘.bib’ file is written next to the current buffer (as
     ‘ab<NUMBER>.bib’) rather than in a ‘mab<NUMBER>/’ subdirectory.
     Second, the inserted block keeps the same two file links (the
     per-note Org file and the chosen article or book PDF) inside an Org
     ‘BEGIN_COMMENT/END_COMMENT’ block, so they do not appear in the
     exported document.

 -- Command: mab-org-wrap-region start end
     Apply ‘mab-org-wrap-citekey’ to every citation in the active
     region.  Citations are processed from end to beginning so that
     earlier positions remain valid as later citations expand.  The
     command asks for confirmation when the region holds more than
     ‘mab-org-region-confirm-threshold’ citations.

 -- Command: mab-org-insert-matching-keys term
     Read a search TERM, scan ‘mab-org-global-bib-file’, and insert one
     ‘[cite:@CITEKEY]’ line at point for every BibTeX entry whose raw
     text contains the term (case insensitive).  Returns the list of
     citekeys, so the function is also useful from other Lisp.

 -- Command: mab-org-add-bib-item &optional file-path
     When called from ‘ebib’'s index buffer, append the wrapped LaTeX
     block for the current entry to a mab project Org file.  The wrapped
     block lands under the section ‘\section*{Illustrated and annotated
     bibliography}’, before any ‘\section*{Backmatter}’ section if one
     is present, or at the end of the file otherwise.  The per-citekey
     abibnote is also created when it does not already exist.

     The destination defaults to ‘mab-org-mab-path’; the most recent
     choice is remembered for the rest of the session.

     When ‘ebib’ is loaded, the command is bound to ‘B’ in
     ‘ebib-index-mode-map’.


